Share the per-client lifetime and ownership infrastructure used by the NPU
backends. The reference-counted context may outlive the file. It protects
hardware quiesce and delayed userspace-VMA teardown. Small callbacks retain
each backend's distinct command-pool and ABI behavior.

This removes the R2P0 exclusive-open restriction without copying the R2P1
close worker. Jobs, imported buffers, memory allocations, mappings, tick
requests and SRAM policy are owned by the common context, so closing one
client cannot reuse a stale struct file address or clear another client's
state. R2P0 mappings retain the owner until the final VMA closes and then
release the complete allocation rather than a mapped subrange.

